      "text": "To solve this problem, we need to determine the length of the tunnel. We start by understanding the given information and the relationships between the variables.\n\n1. **Determine the time it takes for the train to travel from the point where it enters the tunnel to the point where it exits the tunnel:**\n   The train exits the tunnel 3 minutes after the front of the train enters the tunnel. Therefore, the time taken by the train to travel the entire length of the tunnel is 3 minutes.\n\n2. **Convert the time from minutes to hours:**\n   Since there are 60 minutes in an hour, we convert 3 minutes to hours:\n   \\[\n   3 \\text{ minutes} = \\frac{3}{60} \\text{ hours} = \\frac{1}{20} \\text{ hours}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Determine the speed of the train in miles per hour:**\n   The train is moving at a speed of 60 miles per hour.\n\n4. **Calculate the length of the train:**\n   The length of the train is given as 1 mile. However, this information is not needed to solve for the length of the tunnel.\n\n5. **Use the speed formula to find the distance traveled:**\n   The distance traveled by the train in 3 minutes is the product of its speed and the time taken:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Distance} = \\text{Speed} \\times \\text{Time}\n   \\]\n   Substituting the given values:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Distance} = 60 \\text{ miles per hour} \\times \\frac{1}{20} \\text{ hours} = 3 \\text{ miles}\n   \\]\n\n6. **Determine the length of the tunnel:**\n   The distance traveled by the train (3 miles) is equal to the length of the tunnel, because the front of the train enters the tunnel and exits the tunnel 3 minutes later.\n\nTherefore, the length of the tunnel is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{3}\n\\] miles.",
